            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p8-q41-introduction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The division algorithm stops when ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\deg R(x)<\\deg D(x)",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". If you didn’t stop, but rather kept going, you can get some interesting results.",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "break"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The quotient will no longer be a polynomial, but rather, an infinite sum of terms with negative powers.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p8-q41-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "By not terminating the division algorithm, show that:",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p8-q41-convergence",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For these infinite sums, take ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "|x|>1",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p8-q41-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac1x+\\frac1{x^2}+\\frac1{x^3}+\\frac1{x^4}+\\frac1{x^5}+\\cdots$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac1x+\\frac1{x^2}+\\frac1{x^3}+\\cdots$, for $|x|>1$.\n\nContinue dividing $1$ by $x-1$; each successive quotient term is the next negative power of $x$.",
          "worked": "Divide by $x-1$, matching the leading term and subtracting at each stage:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n1-(x-1)x^{-1}&=x^{-1}\\\\\nx^{-1}-(x-1)x^{-2}&=x^{-2}\\\\\nx^{-2}-(x-1)x^{-3}&=x^{-3}\\\\\nx^{-3}-(x-1)x^{-4}&=x^{-4}\\\\\nx^{-4}-(x-1)x^{-5}&=x^{-5}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe quotient terms are $x^{-1},x^{-2},x^{-3},\\ldots$. After $n$ terms the remainder is $x^{-n}$, so the remaining fraction tends to zero when $|x|>1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac1{x-1}&=\\frac1x+\\frac1{x^2}+\\frac1{x^3}+\\frac1{x^4}+\\frac1{x^5}+\\cdots.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q41-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{x}{x^2+1}=\\frac1x-\\frac1{x^3}+\\frac1{x^5}-\\frac1{x^7}+\\frac1{x^9}-\\cdots$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac{x}{x^2+1}=\\frac1x-\\frac1{x^3}+\\frac1{x^5}-\\cdots$, for $|x|>1$.\n\nContinue dividing $x$ by $x^2+1$; the quotient has alternating signs and successive odd negative powers.",
          "worked": "Divide by $x^2+1$, matching the leading term and subtracting at each stage:\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx-(x^2+1)x^{-1}&=-x^{-1}\\\\\n-x^{-1}-(x^2+1)(-x^{-3})&=x^{-3}\\\\\nx^{-3}-(x^2+1)x^{-5}&=-x^{-5}\\\\\n-x^{-5}-(x^2+1)(-x^{-7})&=x^{-7}\\\\\nx^{-7}-(x^2+1)x^{-9}&=-x^{-9}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe quotient terms are $x^{-1},-x^{-3},x^{-5},\\ldots$. The remaining fraction tends to zero when $|x|>1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{x}{x^2+1}&=\\frac1x-\\frac1{x^3}+\\frac1{x^5}-\\frac1{x^7}+\\frac1{x^9}-\\cdots.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q41-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$\\displaystyle\\frac1{(x+1)^2}=\\frac1{x^2}-\\frac2{x^3}+\\frac3{x^4}-\\frac4{x^5}+\\cdots$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\frac1{(x+1)^2}=\\frac1{x^2}-\\frac2{x^3}+\\frac3{x^4}-\\frac4{x^5}+\\cdots$, for $|x|>1$.\n\nContinue dividing $1$ by $x^2+2x+1$; the quotient coefficients alternate in sign with magnitudes $1,2,3,\\ldots$.",
          "worked": "Use $D(x)=(x+1)^2=x^2+2x+1$. Match the leading term and subtract at each stage:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n1-D(x)x^{-2}&=-2x^{-1}-x^{-2}\\\\\n(-2x^{-1}-x^{-2})-D(x)(-2x^{-3})&=3x^{-2}+2x^{-3}\\\\\n(3x^{-2}+2x^{-3})-D(x)(3x^{-4})&=-4x^{-3}-3x^{-4}\\\\\n(-4x^{-3}-3x^{-4})-D(x)(-4x^{-5})&=5x^{-4}+4x^{-5}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe quotient terms are $x^{-2},-2x^{-3},3x^{-4},-4x^{-5},\\ldots$. After $n$ terms the remainder is\n$$\\begin{align*}\nR_n(x)&=(-1)^n\\big((n+1)x^{-n}+nx^{-(n+1)}\\big).\n\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\frac{R_n(x)}{D(x)}$ tends to zero when $|x|>1$, giving\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac1{(x+1)^2}&=\\frac1{x^2}-\\frac2{x^3}+\\frac3{x^4}-\\frac4{x^5}+\\cdots.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      }
    ]
  },
